        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Founded in 1526 CE by Zahir-ud-din Babur following his victory at the First Battle of Panipat, the Mughal Empire forged a centralized pan-Indian state that dominated South Asia for over two centuries. Under Akbar the Great (1556–1605 CE), the empire institutionalized the Mansabdari administrative-military hierarchy, Todar Mal's Dahsala land revenue assessment, and a composite cultural ethos based on Sulh-i-Kul (universal peace), culminating in the architectural zenith under Shah Jahan."
