        "text": "Due to its long coastline, low-lying river deltas, and peninsular proximity to the cyclonically active Bay of Bengal, Andhra Pradesh is among India's most hazard-prone states. The state confronts a dual hazard spectrum: recurrent destructive tropical cyclones, storm surges, and riverine floods in coastal districts, alongside persistent chronic droughts in interior Rayalaseema. In response, the Andhra Pradesh State Disaster Management Authority (APSDMA) has pioneered coastal shelters, real-time Doppler radar early warning, and disaster mitigation models."
