    "content": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 1,
        "text": "Inclusive Growth, Poverty, Unemployment & SDGs"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Inclusive growth ensures that the fruits of economic expansion are broadly distributed across all strata of society, generating equitable opportunities and reducing deep-rooted structural disparities. In India, poverty estimation has evolved from basic calorie-consumption expenditure thresholds to multidimensional deprivation assessments. Concurrently, employment measurement through the Periodic Labour Force Survey (PLFS) and performance tracking through the NITI Aayog SDG India Index guide state and national social investments."
      },
      {
        "type": "image",
        "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Sustainable_Development_Goals.svg",
        "caption": "The 17 United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), monitored across Indian States by NITI Aayog.",
        "alt_text": "Sustainable Development Goals overview"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "1. Macroeconomic Framework & Indicators"
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "caption": "Evolution of Poverty Estimation Methodologies in India",
        "headers": [
          "Expert Group / Committee",
          "Poverty Benchmark Criterion",
          "Rural / Urban Thresholds",
          "Salient Innovations & Methodological Shift"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Lakdawala Committee (1993)",
            "Calorie norms (2400 rural / 2100 urban)",
            "State-specific CPI-AL & CPI-IW pricing",
            "Disaggregated poverty lines across states; relied on URP NSS consumption surveys"
          ],
          [
            "Suresh Tendulkar Committee (2009)",
            "Uniform Poverty Line Basket (PLB) based on urban consumption",
            "Rs 816 rural / Rs 1,000 urban per capita/month (2011-12)",
            "Moved away from calorie anchor; incorporated private expenditure on health and education"
          ],
          [
            "C. Rangarajan Committee (2014)",
            "Independent nutritional + essential non-food expenditure basket",
            "Rs 972 rural / Rs 1,407 urban per capita/month (2011-12)",
            "Calculated family expenditure for 5 members; estimated 29.5% poverty vs Tendulkar's 21.9%"
          ],
          [
            "NITI Aayog National MPI (2021-23)",
            "Alkire-Foster method across Health, Education, Standard of Living (12 indicators)",
            "Multidimensionally poor if deprived in >= 33.3% of weighted indicators",
            "Non-income multidimensional measure: reported 13.5 crore Indians exited poverty between 2015-16 & 2019-21"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "2. Key Policy Dimensions & Analytical Insights"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "style": "bullet",
        "items": [
          "National Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI): Formulated by NITI Aayog using NFHS microdata across 3 equally weighted dimensions (Health, Education, Standard of Living) and 12 indicators (including nutrition, child-adolescent mortality, maternal health, cooking fuel, sanitation, drinking water, electricity, housing, assets, bank accounts).",
          "Unemployment Measurements under PLFS: Conducted by NSO since 2017. Measures Labor Force Participation Rate (LFPR), Worker Population Ratio (WPR), and Unemployment Rate (UR) using Usual Status (ps+ss, 365-day recall) and Current Weekly Status (CWS, 7-day recall).",
          "Structural vs Frictional vs Disguised Unemployment: India's predominant labor market challenge is disguised unemployment in agriculture (marginal productivity of labor approaching zero) alongside youth educated unemployment driven by skill mismatch.",
          "SDG India Index Architecture: Developed by NITI Aayog to monitor national and sub-national progress across 17 SDGs. Classifies States and UTs into four categories: Achiever (100), Front Runner (65–99), Performer (50–64), and Aspirant (0–49).",
          "Safety Nets and Social Security Innovations: Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT) via the JAM Trinity (Jan Dhan, Aadhaar, Mobile), PM Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana (PMGKAY), and MGNREGS have established a resilient universal social safety net mitigating consumption shocks."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Previous Years Questions (PYQs) - APPSC Group-1"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Part A: Mains Analytical & Descriptive Questions"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "style": "numbered",
        "items": [
          "Explain how NITI Aayog's National Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI) differs from traditional consumption-expenditure poverty lines. Discuss the key drivers behind India's multidimensional poverty reduction in recent years. (250 Words, 15 Marks)",
          "Analyze the structural bottlenecks facing female labor force participation (FLFPR) in India. What targeted policy interventions are needed to boost women's formal employment? (200 Words, 10 Marks)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Part B: Screening / Prelims Objective Questions"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "style": "bullet",
        "items": [
          "**Q: Which committee recommended shifting from calorie-consumption based poverty lines to a uniform poverty line basket incorporating health and education expenses?**\n* (a) Alagh Committee\n* (b) Lakdawala Committee\n* (c) Suresh Tendulkar Committee\n* (d) C. Rangarajan Committee\n* **Answer: (c)**\n* *Explanation:* The Suresh Tendulkar Committee (submitted in 2009) departed from the earlier calorie-norm approach and formulated poverty lines incorporating explicit spending on education and healthcare.",
          "**Q: How many indicators across how many dimensions are evaluated in NITI Aayog's National Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI)?**\n* (a) 10 indicators across 3 dimensions\n* (b) 12 indicators across 3 dimensions\n* (c) 15 indicators across 4 dimensions\n* (d) 17 indicators across 5 dimensions\n* **Answer: (b)**\n* *Explanation:* NITI Aayog's National MPI evaluates 12 indicators across 3 equally weighted dimensions: Health (Nutrition, Child-Adolescent Mortality, Maternal Health), Education (Years of Schooling, School Attendance), and Standard of Living (Cooking Fuel, Sanitation, Drinking Water, Housing, Electricity, Assets, Bank Accounts).",
          "**Q: In the Periodic Labour Force Survey (PLFS), how is the 'Unemployment Rate (UR)' officially defined?**\n* (a) Percentage of total population without employment\n* (b) Percentage of persons unemployed among the persons in the labour force\n* (c) Percentage of working-age population without jobs\n* (d) Number of registered applicants in employment exchanges\n* **Answer: (b)**\n* *Explanation:* Unemployment Rate (UR) is strictly defined as the percentage of unemployed persons among the persons in the labour force (Labour Force = Employed + Unemployed).",
          "**Q: In the NITI Aayog SDG India Index classification, a state scoring between 65 and 99 points is categorized under which tier?**\n* (a) Aspirant\n* (b) Performer\n* (c) Front Runner\n* (d) Achiever\n* **Answer: (c)**\n* *Explanation:* States scoring 65–99 on the NITI Aayog SDG India Index are categorized as 'Front Runners'. Scores 50–64 are 'Performers', below 50 are 'Aspirants', and 100 represents 'Achievers'.",
          "**Q: What phenomenon occurs in agriculture when more workers are engaged than practically required, such that the marginal productivity of additional labor is near zero?**\n* (a) Frictional Unemployment\n* (b) Cyclical Unemployment\n* (c) Disguised Unemployment\n* (d) Structural Unemployment\n* **Answer: (c)**\n* *Explanation:* Disguised unemployment is characterized by redundant labor in a sector (particularly agrarian family farming) where surplus workers contribute zero marginal productivity to the total output."
        ]
      }
    ],
