        "text": "India is one of the world's 17 megadiverse nations, harboring four global biodiversity hotspots (Western Ghats, Himalayas, Indo-Burma, Sundaland). Environmental governance rests on robust constitutional mandates (Articles 48A and 51A(g)) operationalized through pioneering statutory frameworks: the Wildlife (Protection) Act 1972, the Environment (Protection) Act 1986, the Biological Diversity Act 2002, and the specialized National Green Tribunal (NGT). Modern amendments harmonize wildlife conservation with CITES treaty compliance, access and benefit sharing (ABS), and sustainable developmental imperatives."
