          "Q1. Statements:\n1. All IAS officers are administrators.\n2. Some administrators are authors.\nConclusions:\nI. Some IAS officers are authors.\nII. Some authors are administrators.\n(a) Only Conclusion I follows\n(b) Only Conclusion II follows\n(c) Both Conclusions I and II follow\n(d) Neither Conclusion I nor II follows\nCorrect Answer: (b) Only Conclusion II follows [Explanation: 'All IAS are administrators' (A) and 'Some administrators are authors' (I). Middle term 'administrators' is undistributed in both premises, so no categorical conclusion links IAS officers with authors. However, premise 2 'Some administrators are authors' converts simply by conversion to 'Some authors are administrators'. Thus only Conclusion II follows.]",
          "Q2. Statements: 'Should diesel luxury SUVs be subjected to a 50% environmental cess in metro cities?'\nArguments:\nI. Yes, because diesel emissions contribute significantly to hazardous PM2.5 particulates and toxic air pollution in dense urban clusters.\nII. No, because luxury vehicles are driven by wealthy individuals who contribute high direct income taxes to the government.\n(a) Only Argument I is strong\n(b) Only Argument II is strong\n(c) Both Arguments I and II are strong\n(d) Neither Argument I nor II is strong\nCorrect Answer: (a) Only Argument I is strong [Explanation: Argument I is strong as it provides a direct, factual environmental and public health justification. Argument II is weak and flawed because paying income tax does not grant an exemption from environmental accountability under the Polluter Pays Principle.]",
          "Q3. In a row of 40 students facing North, P is 13th from the left end and Q is 9th from the right end. How many students are seated between P and Q?\n(a) 16\n(b) 18\n(c) 19\n(d) 20\nCorrect Answer: (b) 18 [Explanation: Total students = 40. P is 13th from left, Q is 9th from right. Sum of their end positions = 13 + 9 = 22. Since total (40) > sum (22), there is no overlap. Students between P and Q = Total - (Left rank + Right rank) = 40 - (13 + 9) = 40 - 22 = 18.]",
          "Q4. In a code language, if 'NOIDA' is coded as '39658', how is 'INDIA' coded?\n(a) 63568\n(b) 63586\n(c) 65628\n(d) 36568\nCorrect Answer: (a) 63568 [Explanation: Comparing corresponding letters in NOIDA (N=3, O=9, I=6, D=5, A=8). For INDIA: I=6, N=3, D=5, I=6, A=8, giving 63568.]",
          "Q5. Seven persons A, B, C, D, E, F, and G are sitting in a straight row facing North. C is sitting fourth to the right of G. Neither C nor G sits at any extreme end. A is sitting second to the left of C. Who is sitting exactly at the center (4th position) of the row?\n(a) A\n(b) B\n(c) D\n(d) C\nCorrect Answer: (a) A [Explanation: In a row of 7 seats (1 to 7). G and C are not at ends, and C is 4th to the right of G (meaning C index = G index + 4). Since G >= 2 and C <= 6: G must be at seat 2, which places C at seat 2 + 4 = 6. A is 2nd to the left of C, so A is at seat 6 - 2 = 4 (the exact center of the row).] "
