    "topic_title": "Data Interpretation, Data Sufficiency & Elementary Probability",
    "syllabus_mapping": "Data interpretation, Data Analysis, Data sufficiency, and concepts of Probability - Tabular Charts, Bar Graphs, Line Graphs, Pie Charts (Degree to Percentage Conversion), Data Sufficiency Decision Logic, Classical and Empirical Probability, Permutations and Combinations Basics.",
    "last_updated": "2026-10",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 1,
        "text": "Data Interpretation (DI), Data Sufficiency Frameworks, and Probability Theory"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Data Interpretation assesses the quantitative ability to synthesize tabular and graphic data into statistical conclusions. Data Sufficiency evaluates whether provided statements provide sufficient mathematical information to answer a question without completing tedious full computations."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "1. Data Interpretation Visualizations & Conversions"
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "caption": "Key Visual Formats in Data Interpretation",
        "headers": [
          "Chart Type",
          "Visual Nature",
          "Key Conversion Formula / Method"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Pie Chart",
            "Circular sector distribution totaling $360^\\circ$ or $100\\%$.",
            "$\\text{Percentage} = \\frac{\\text{Angle}}{360^\\circ} \\times 100\\%$; $\\text{Angle} = \\frac{\\text{Percentage}}{100} \\times 360^\\circ$."
          ],
          [
            "Bar Graph",
            "Discrete rectangular bars comparing categorical magnitudes.",
            "Percentage Growth: $\\frac{\\text{Value}_{t} - \\text{Value}_{t-1}}{\\text{Value}_{t-1}} \\times 100\\%$."
          ],
          [
            "Line Graph",
            "Continuous time-series tracking temporal trends.",
            "Identify rate of change / slope of line segments."
          ],
          [
            "Tabular Data",
            "Multi-dimensional rows and columns.",
            "Ratio comparisons and column aggregations."
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "2. Data Sufficiency Decision Matrix"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "style": "bullet",
        "items": [
          "Option A: Statement (I) ALONE is sufficient to answer the question, but Statement (II) alone is not sufficient.",
          "Option B: Statement (II) ALONE is sufficient to answer the question, but Statement (I) alone is not sufficient.",
          "Option C: BOTH Statements (I) and (II) TOGETHER are required to answer the question.",
          "Option D: Statements (I) and (II) TOGETHER are NOT sufficient to answer the question (additional data needed).",
          "Option E: EITHER Statement (I) alone OR Statement (II) alone is sufficient independently."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "3. Probability Fundamentals"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Probability is the measure of the likelihood that an event will occur: $P(E) = \\frac{n(E)}{n(S)}$, where $n(E)$ is the number of favorable outcomes and $n(S)$ is the total sample space. $0 \\le P(E) \\le 1$."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Previous Years Questions (PYQs) - APPSC Group-1"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Part A: Analytical Data Sufficiency Evaluation"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "style": "numbered",
        "items": [
          "Question: Is the integer x divisible by 6?\nStatement I: x is divisible by 3.\nStatement II: x is divisible by 4.\nEvaluate the data sufficiency step-by-step and determine whether statements alone or together suffice. (APPSC Group-1 Mains Analytical, 10 Marks)\n-> Model Approach: To be divisible by 6, x must be divisible by both 2 and 3. Statement I tells us x is divisible by 3 (e.g., x=9, not divisible by 6; x=12, divisible by 6) -> Insufficient. Statement II tells us x is divisible by 4 (meaning x is divisible by 2 and an even multiple of 4; e.g., x=8, not divisible by 6; x=12, divisible by 6) -> Insufficient. Combining Statements I and II: x is divisible by both 3 and 4. Since 3 and 4 are co-prime, x must be divisible by LCM(3, 4) = 12. Every multiple of 12 is necessarily a multiple of 6 (12 = 6 * 2). Thus, both statements together are SUFFICIENT to answer the question definitively."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Part B: Screening / Prelims Objective Questions"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "style": "bullet",
        "items": [
          "Q1. Two dice are thrown simultaneously. What is the probability of getting two numbers whose sum is at least 10?\n(a) 1/6\n(b) 1/12\n(c) 5/36\n(d) 1/4\nCorrect Answer: (a) 1/6 [Explanation: Total outcomes n(S) = 6 * 6 = 36. Outcomes with sum >= 10: Sum 10: (4,6), (5,5), (6,4) [3]. Sum 11: (5,6), (6,5) [2]. Sum 12: (6,6) [1]. Total favorable outcomes n(E) = 3 + 2 + 1 = 6. Probability = 6 / 36 = 1/6.]",
          "Q2. In a pie chart showing government budgetary expenditures, the sector for 'Education' subtends a central angle of 54°. What percentage of total budget is spent on Education?\n(a) 12%\n(b) 15%\n(c) 18%\n(d) 20%\nCorrect Answer: (b) 15% [Explanation: Percentage = (Angle / 360°) * 100% = (54 / 360) * 100 = (3 / 20) * 100 = 15%.]",
          "Q3. From a standard pack of 52 cards, two cards are drawn at random. What is the probability that both cards are Kings?\n(a) 1/169\n(b) 1/221\n(c) 4/663\n(d) 2/221\nCorrect Answer: (b) 1/221 [Explanation: P = [4C2] / [52C2] = [ (4 * 3) / 2 ] / [ (52 * 51) / 2 ] = 6 / 1326 = 1 / 221.]",
          "Q4. A bag contains 5 red, 4 green, and 3 blue balls. If a ball is drawn at random, what is the probability that it is NOT green?\n(a) 1/3\n(b) 2/3\n(c) 3/4\n(d) 5/12\nCorrect Answer: (b) 2/3 [Explanation: Total balls = 5 + 4 + 3 = 12. Non-green balls = Red + Blue = 5 + 3 = 8. Probability = 8 / 12 = 2/3.]",
          "Q5. Question: What is the age of Ravi?\nStatement I: Ravi is 4 years older than his sister Priya.\nStatement II: The ratio of Ravi's age to Priya's age is 5:3.\nWhich statement(s) are sufficient?\n(a) Statement I alone is sufficient\n(b) Statement II alone is sufficient\n(c) Both Statements I and II together are sufficient\n(d) Neither Statement is sufficient\nCorrect Answer: (c) Both Statements I and II together are sufficient [Explanation: Statement I alone: R - P = 4 (infinite possibilities). Statement II alone: R/P = 5/3 (infinite possibilities). Combining both: 5x - 3x = 4 => 2x = 4 => x = 2. Ravi's age = 5(2) = 10 years. Both statements together are sufficient.]"
        ]
      }
    ],
