    "raw_text": "Statement: 'To reduce urban traffic congestion and vehicular emissions, the municipal corporation proposes a heavy congestion fee on private vehicles entering the central business district during peak hours.'\nEvaluate the following courses of action as a senior administrator:\nCourse of Action I: The municipal corporation should implement the congestion fee immediately without waiting for alternative public transit arrangements.\nCourse of Action II: The municipal corporation should significantly increase high-frequency electric bus shuttles and metro feeders connecting suburban parking hubs prior to or simultaneously with levying congestion fees.\nCourse of Action III: The municipal corporation should completely ban all private vehicles from entering the city permanently.\n(APPSC Group-1 Mains Analytical, 10 Marks)\n-> Model Approach: Course of Action I is unviable because punitive pricing without viable alternatives creates acute citizen distress and economic stagnation. Course of Action II is the correct and balanced administrative intervention because economic disincentives must be matched with reliable public transport alternatives to achieve sustainable modal shift. Course of Action III is disproportionate, extreme, and unconstitutional, as an outright ban violates freedom of movement and cripples commercial activity."
