    "topic_title": "Central Tendencies: Mean, Median, Mode & Weighted Averages",
    "subject_title": "General Mental and Psychological Abilities",
    "raw_text": "In a moderately skewed district health examination dataset, the mean hemoglobin level is recorded as 13.2 g/dL and the median hemoglobin level is 12.8 g/dL. Determine the mode of the distribution using Karl Pearson's empirical formula. Discuss why median is preferred over mean in reporting household per capita incomes. (APPSC Group-1 Mains Analytical, 10 Marks)\n-> Model Approach: 1. Empirical Formula: Mode = 3*Median - 2*Mean. Mode = 3(12.8) - 2(13.2) = 38.4 - 26.4 = 12.0 g/dL. 2. Median vs. Mean for Household Incomes: Income distribution in developing economies is heavily right-skewed with extreme high-income billionaires at the tail. The arithmetic mean is disproportionately inflated by these outliers, creating an overly optimistic false impression of prosperity. The median represents the exact 50th percentile, unaffected by extreme fortunes, reflecting the authentic economic welfare of typical citizens."
