    "raw_text": "Prove that the quadrilateral formed by joining the mid-points of the sides of any arbitrary quadrilateral is always a parallelogram (Varignon's Theorem). Formulate the proof using triangle mid-point theorems. (APPSC Group-1 Mains Analytical, 10 Marks)\n-> Model Approach: Let ABCD be an arbitrary quadrilateral and P, Q, R, S be the mid-points of sides AB, BC, CD, and DA respectively. Join diagonal AC.\n1. In triangle ABC: P is mid-point of AB, Q is mid-point of BC. By Mid-point Theorem, PQ is parallel to AC and PQ = (1/2) AC.\n2. In triangle ADC: S is mid-point of AD, R is mid-point of CD. By Mid-point Theorem, SR is parallel to AC and SR = (1/2) AC.\n3. From (1) and (2): PQ is parallel to SR, and PQ = SR.\n4. A quadrilateral with one pair of opposite sides both equal and parallel is by definition a parallelogram. Therefore, PQRS is a parallelogram."
